<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Chronicleaf.Infrastructure</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Chronicleaf.Infrastructure</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Chronicleaf.Application/Chronicleaf.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Chronicleaf.Core/Chronicleaf.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Binder" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" Version="10.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Durable execution (BACKGROUND-JOBS-001). Versions are pinned exactly, and each was restored
      from nuget.org and run against the real PostgreSQL the tests use before being written here.

      Hangfire.PostgreSql 1.21.1 is the newest version published for the PostgreSQL storage. It
      targets netstandard2.0 and declares Npgsql 6.0.11, which NuGet unifies to the 10.0.3 the EF
      provider already brings; the probe that runs the queue against PostgreSQL 17 through that
      unified Npgsql is part of the integration suite, so the unification is exercised rather than
      assumed.

      Newtonsoft.Json is pinned to a patched 13.0.3. Hangfire.PostgreSql's own transitive floor is
      11.0.1, which carries a known high-severity advisory (GHSA-5crp-9r3c-p9vr); without this
      reference restore emits NU1903 and the build would carry a known-vulnerable serializer.

      Hangfire.Core moves 1.8.21 -> 1.8.23, the version Platform.Jobs.Hangfire pins, so the adapter and
    this project never hold two copies of the engine. Hangfire.PostgreSql stays at 1.21.1 and is kept
    as a direct reference because the queue's storage is PostgreSQL and that choice is Chronicleaf's,
    not the platform adapter's. The dashboard integration (Hangfire.AspNetCore/Hangfire.NetCore) is
    still NOT referenced: BackgroundJobsTests asserts this assembly's referenced set, so a dashboard
    that cannot be mapped is a stronger property than a dashboard that is merely switched off.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Hangfire.Core" Version="1.8.23" />
    <PackageReference Include="Hangfire.PostgreSql" Version="1.21.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Adopted durable scheduling (PLATFORM-JOBS-ADOPTION-001/002). The dispatcher contract, the
      payload record and the Hangfire adapter are the shared platform's, taken by exact version from
      the .nupkg files committed under packages/platform-feed (Ruling 12), for the same reason the
      storage packages below are: no sibling checkout, no absolute path, no environment-variable feed.

      Platform.Jobs 0.1.0 owns IJobDispatcher, JobPayload and the IJobPayloadHandler's payload shape.
      Platform.Jobs.Hangfire 0.1.0 owns the engine wiring - HangfireJobDispatcher, HangfireJobExecutor,
      ScopedJobActivator and the storage health check - reached through AddPlatformHangfireJobs. Its
      Hangfire 1.8.23/PostgreSql 1.21.1 pins are the versions adopted above. Platform.Core arrives
      transitively and supplies Platform.Core.Time.IClock.

      This remains the ONLY project that needs the feed: Core, Application and Api compile without it,
      which is why the operation state machine in Application keeps its own narrow retry seam while
      every platform-typed seam - dispatcher, payload handler, executor, clock, server - lives here.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Platform.Jobs" Version="0.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Platform.Jobs.Hangfire" Version="0.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Adopted shared storage (LOCAL-MEDIA-STORAGE-001/002). Ruling 12 of the workspace
      ARCHITECTURE.md is what makes these two references legitimate: the versions are pinned
      exactly, and the artifacts they resolve from are the .nupkg files committed under
      packages/platform-feed, reached through the repo-relative `chronicleaf-platform-feed`
      source in nuget.config. There is no ProjectReference to a sibling checkout, no absolute
      path and no environment-variable feed, so `dotnet build` succeeds on a clone that has no
      sibling at all. scripts/pack-platform.sh re-packs the feed and is never part of the build.

      Platform.Storage 0.1.0 owns IObjectStorage, StorageObjectKey (which already refuses
      traversal, empty segments, control characters and an over-long key), StorageOptions,
      StorageOutcome and the provider status contract. Platform.Storage.Local 0.1.0 is the
      filesystem adapter this package is scoped to; Platform.Core arrives transitively and is
      therefore committed to the feed but not referenced here.

      This is the ONLY project that needs the feed. Core, Application and Api compile without
      it, which is what keeps the layering honest: the storage interface belongs to the shared
      platform, and only the adapter that implements it is an Infrastructure concern.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Platform.Storage" Version="0.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Platform.Storage.Local" Version="0.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Design-time only: required by `dotnet ef` but never referenced by the runtime. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.4">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
